{'id': '2608.00559', 'categories': ['cs.CV'], 'pdf': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.00559', 'abs': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00559', 'authors': ['Yiqian Zhang', 'Zheyuan Gu', 'Xiangzhao Hao', 'Zefeng Zhang', 'Jingjia Mao', 'Jiahao Hu', 'Jiaxu Miao', 'Jun Yu', 'Zhenyu Zhang', 'Shuohuan Wang', 'Yu Sun'], 'title': 'Test-Time Curriculum for Open-Set AIGC Detection', 'comment': None, 'summary': 'AI-generated image detectors deployed in open-world environments inevitably face distribution shifts as new and stronger generative models continue to emerge. Although existing methods improve cross-generator generalization through better representations or training data construction, they typically follow a static train-once-and-deploy paradigm and cannot adapt after deployment. In this work, we study open-set AIGC image detection from a test-time adaptation perspective. We propose Test-Time Curriculum (TTC), a simple and model-agnostic framework that adapts a detector on unlabeled test data through curriculum-based self-training. TTC starts from highly reliable pseudo-labeled samples and progressively incorporates harder yet informative cases, while enforcing class-balanced selection to reduce biased updates under generator shift. To further improve pseudo-label quality, we introduce Cross-Scale Pseudo-Label Refinement, which aggregates complementary evidence across multiple resolutions for more reliable adaptation, and applies noisy-or fusion at inference to strengthen final predictions. In addition, we construct AIGCGuard, a new benchmark containing 3,100 representative real images and 124,000 generated images from 40 of the most advanced open-source and proprietary text-to-image models. Extensive experiments on five benchmarks show that TTC substantially improves overall detection performance under diverse unseen-generator shifts, establishing a practical and effective test-time adaptation framework for open-set generated image detection.'}
